Unsigned shift-add <p>For \(n\)-bit operands:</p> <pre><code>product = 0
for i in 0..n-1:
    if multiplier[i] == 1:
        product += multiplicand &lt;&lt; i
</code></pre> <p>Takes \(n\) cycles; the multiplier is examined bit by bit.</p>   ??? warning "Limitation"     This is for **unsigned** operands. For signed operands you would need to (a) take magnitudes, (b) multiply, (c) apply the sign. Booth's algorithm avoids this.

The algorithm <p><pre><code>while (!(status &amp; READY)) ;
data = device.data;
</code></pre> The CPU busy-waits in a loop, checking the status register.</p>   ???+ info "Cost"     - 100% CPU utilisation while waiting.     - Simple to implement.     - Only acceptable when the device is **very fast** (ready on the next cycle) or the CPU has nothing else to do.     !!! warning "When programmed I/O is a mistake"     For a 10 ms disk, the CPU wastes 10⁷ cycles polling — billions of wasted cycles per second if the disk is active.
